{"cuisine/thai-american-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/thai-american-cuisine","n":"Thai-American cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in United States of America","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Thai-American cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"thai-american-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thai-American_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Night + Market: Delicious Thai Food to Facilitate Drinking and Fun-Having Amongst Friends","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Kris Yenbamroong with Garrett Snyder, 2017","note":"Thai cooking from the Los Angeles restaurant run by a Thai American chef.","url":"https://penguinrandomhouse.com/books/547465/night--market-by-kris-yenbamroong","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780451497871"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/austrian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/austrian-cuisine","n":"Austrian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Austria","intro":[["Austrian cuisine consists of many different local or regional cuisines. In addition to Viennese cuisine, which is predominantly based on the cooking traditions of the Habsburg Empire, there are independent regional traditions in all the states of Austria."],["The Austrian cuisine shares similarities with its neighboring countries in Central Europe, but particularly with the cuisines of Hungary (with which it was aligned as part of the Austro-Hungarian Empire), Bavaria, Bohemia and Northern Italy. Internationally, it is particularly well-known for its pastries, such as Kaiserschmarrn and apple strudel, and for meat-based dishes such as the Wiener schnitzel."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"austrian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austrian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":15,"links":[["Blini","dish/blini"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chicken paprikash","dish/chicken-paprikash"],["Dampfnudel","dish/dampfnudel"],["Egg puffs and soup pearls","dish/egg-puffs-and-soup-pearls"],["Gulyásleves","dish/gulyasleves"],["Leberkäse","dish/leberkase"],["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"],["Pancake","dish/pancake"],["Potato salad","dish/potato-salad"],["Sachertorte","dish/sachertorte"],["Spätzle","dish/spatzle"],["Strudel","dish/strudel"],["Tarhonya","dish/tarhonya"],["Zopf","dish/zopf"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Vienna Sunday Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vienna-sunday.kitchen","note":"Viennese food blog of Austrian home cooking and pastries, with English-language recipes.","url":"https://www.vienna-sunday.kitchen/newest-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Lil Vienna","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lilvienna.com","note":"Austrian recipes from Ursula and David, two Austrians living in Boston.","url":"https://www.lilvienna.com/?p=1366","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Austrian Cooking and Baking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Gretel Beer","note":"Long-standing English-language classic on Austrian dishes and baking, in print from Dover.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780486232201","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780486232201"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Jam","ingredient/jam"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Margarine","ingredient/margarine"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Bryndza","ingredient/bryndza"],["Cottage cheese","ingredient/cottage-cheese"],["Goat cheese","ingredient/goat-cheese"],["Quark","ingredient/quark"],["Quark cheese","ingredient/quark-cheese"],["Sheep milk","ingredient/sheep-milk"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Austrian cooking","count":1,"links":[["Viennese cuisine","cuisine/viennese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/39/Grie%C3%9Fnockerlsuppe_cropped.jpg/500px-Grie%C3%9Fnockerlsuppe_cropped.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Christian Michelides","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grie%C3%9Fnockerlsuppe_cropped.jpg"}},"cuisine/moravian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/moravian-cuisine","n":"Moravian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Czech cuisine","cuisine/czech-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Czechia","intro":[["Moravian cuisine encompasses the cooking styles, traditions and recipes associated with Moravia, a region of the Czech Republic (eastern part) and historically belongs to the Moravia, former historical country in Central Europe. Today, it is often perceived as an integral part of Czech cuisine, to which it has over the last century been artificially accommodated and mixed. Nevertheless, there is a large list of dishes, drinks and customs that are original only for Moravia."],["Moravian cuisine includes many pork and poultry meat and knödel dishes (koláčky, gulivary, pěry), and often uses flour, in the south many vegetables and fruits such as plums."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"moravian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moravian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Czech cuisine","cuisine/czech-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Czech cuisine","cuisine/czech-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ae/Stuffed_paprica.jpg/500px-Stuffed_paprica.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Jan Sapák","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stuffed_paprica.jpg"}},"cuisine/coast-salish-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/coast-salish-foodways","n":"Coast Salish foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Puget Sound and the Salish Sea, Washington","names":"Coast Salish peoples, including Muckleshoot and Nisqually","intro":[[["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"],", berries, elk and roots are the core of the Coast Salish diet around the Salish Sea. Muckleshoot guidelines call for a traditional meal like roasted salmon once a week, and intertribal ",["Camas","ingredient/camassia"]," harvests bring Elders and youth together."],["The Muckleshoot Food Sovereignty Project brings huckleberries, nettles, camas, salmon and elk back into everyday meals through classes, gardens and seminars, and cooks try new forms too, like elk burgers and huckleberry smoothies. At Nisqually, a community garden gives citizens free produce and syrups made from nettle, elderberry and fir tips."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Northwest Indian Fisheries Commission, Muckleshoot food program fosters creative solutions","url":"https://nwifc.org/muckleshoot-food-program-fosters-creative-solutions/"},{"name":"Society of Ethnobiology, The Muckleshoot Food Sovereignty Project","url":"https://ethnobiology.org/muckleshoot-food-sovereignty-project-revitalizing-northwest-coastal-indian-food-culture"},{"name":"Cascade PBS, How tribes and local organizations are bolstering food sovereignty","url":"https://www.cascadepbs.org/news/2022/10/how-tribes-local-organizations-are-bolstering-food-sovereignty"},{"name":"Washington State Health Care Authority, Food and survival","url":"https://connections.hca.wa.gov/hca/2025/november/food-and-survival-why-fry-bread-is-not-a-novelty-food"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"coast-salish-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"dish/ajvar":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ajvar","n":"Ajvar","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Balkan cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Ajvar is a condiment made principally from sweet bell peppers and eggplants. The relish became a popular side dish throughout Yugoslavia after World War II and remains popular in Southeast Europe."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ajvar"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajvar"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Balkan cuisine","cuisine/balkan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"]]},{"h":"More from Balkan cuisine","links":[["Ayran","dish/ayran"],["Stuffed tomatoes","dish/stuffed-tomatoes"],["Tarator","dish/tarator"],["Torshi","dish/torshi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/92/Ajvar_toast.jpg/500px-Ajvar_toast.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Popo le Chien","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ajvar_toast.jpg"}},"dish/crozets-de-savoie":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/crozets-de-savoie","n":"Crozets de Savoie","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Crozets de Savoie are small, square-shaped, flat pasta originally made in the Savoie region in southeast France. Crozets were made traditionally at home by housewives using buckwheat or wheat, or sometimes both. This pasta is used mainly to prepare two regional dishes, the croziflette (a variant of tartiflette) and crozets with diots (traditional Savoyard sausage)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-crozets-de-savoie"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crozets_de_Savoie"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Buckwheat","ingredient/buckwheat"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"New Caledonia Tourism: Kanak cooking","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newcaledonia.travel","note":"Official tourism board overview of Kanak food, including bougna cooked in an earth oven.","url":"https://www.newcaledonia.travel/nz/gastronomy-wine/gastronomy/kanak-cooking","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from French cuisine","links":[["Adventureland Bazaar","dish/adventureland-bazaar"],["Albufera sauce","dish/albufera-sauce"],["Andouillette","dish/andouillette"],["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Bayonne ham","dish/bayonne-ham"],["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"],["Beurre maître d'hôtel","dish/beurre-maitre-d-hotel"],["Beurre manié","dish/beurre-manie"],["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"],["Blanquette de veau","dish/blanquette-de-veau"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bouillabaisse","dish/bouillabaisse"],["Bourride","dish/bourride"],["Brioche","dish/brioche"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Calisson","dish/calisson"],["Canapé","dish/canape"],["Cervelle de canut","dish/cervelle-de-canut"],["Cream of sorrel soup","dish/cream-of-sorrel-soup"],["Crêpes Suzette","dish/crepes-suzette"],["Croquembouche","dish/croquembouche"],["Crudités","dish/crudites"],["Damien Pignolet","dish/damien-pignolet"],["Daube","dish/daube"],["Demi-glace","dish/demi-glace"],["Duxelles","dish/duxelles"],["Egg wash","dish/egg-wash"],["Espagnole sauce","dish/espagnole-sauce"],["Fin gras du Mézenc","dish/fin-gras-du-mezenc"],["Flammekueche","dish/flammekueche"],["Foie gras","dish/foie-gras"],["Foin de Crau","dish/foin-de-crau"],["Fraisier","dish/fraisier"],["French onion soup","dish/french-onion-soup"],["French restaurant","dish/french-restaurant"],["Fricassee","dish/fricassee"],["Garbure","dish/garbure"],["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"],["Gugelhupf","dish/gugelhupf"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/51/Crozets.jpeg/500px-Crozets.jpeg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Popo le Chien","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crozets.jpeg"}},"ingredient/datshi":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/datshi","n":"Datshi","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Datshi is a traditional Bhutanese cottage cheese commonly produced from cow milk. It is a naturally fermented milk product produced using the back-slopping method. To make Datshi, Dahi is first prepared from raw milk, the Dahi is then processed into Mar (artisanal butter) by churning in a special wooden container called Theki. The Mar is collected and the liquid residue, called Mohi, is gently heated. The heating causes casein to coagulate and the Mohi starts forming clumps. The Datshi is collected in a cloth and squeezed to eliminate extra whey and then rolled into appropriate sized cheese balls."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-datshi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Datshi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Bhutanese cuisine","cuisine/bhutanese-cuisine",[["Ema datshi","dish/ema-datshi"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/82/Datshi.jpg/500px-Datshi.jpg","w":500,"h":568,"by":"Nair A, Choden D, Pradhan M.","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Datshi.jpg"}},"dish/finnan-haddie":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/finnan-haddie","n":"Finnan haddie","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Scottish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A Finnan haddie is a haddock that has been cured with the smoke of green wood or peat. They are usually said to have originated in Findon, a fishing village south of Aberdeen, though an alternative tradition traces them to Findhorn in Moray."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-finnan-haddie"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnan_haddie"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Haddock","ingredient/haddock"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Scottish Scran","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"scottishscran.com","note":"Blog working through traditional Scottish dishes one recipe at a time.","url":"https://scottishscran.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Something Sweet Something Savoury: Scottish recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"somethingsweetsomethingsavoury.com","note":"Scottish home baking and cooking section of a UK food blog.","url":"https://somethingsweetsomethingsavoury.com/category/scottish-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Scottish cuisine","links":[["Clootie dumpling","dish/clootie-dumpling"],["Cock-a-leekie","dish/cock-a-leekie"],["Haggis","dish/haggis"],["Haggis pakora","dish/haggis-pakora"],["Hobnob biscuit","dish/hobnob-biscuit"],["Lorne sausage","dish/lorne-sausage"],["Mince and tatties","dish/mince-and-tatties"],["Rumbledethumps","dish/rumbledethumps"],["Scotch broth","dish/scotch-broth"],["Skirlie","dish/skirlie"],["Stornoway black pudding","dish/stornoway-black-pudding"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c3/FMIB_38894_Smoking_Finnan_Haddie.jpeg/500px-FMIB_38894_Smoking_Finnan_Haddie.jpeg","w":500,"h":399,"by":"Charles Hugh Stevenson","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FMIB_38894_Smoking_Finnan_Haddie.jpeg"}},"dish/kotlet-schabowy":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/kotlet-schabowy","n":"Kotlet schabowy","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Polish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Kotlet schabowy is a Polish variety of a breaded cutlet of pork coated with breadcrumbs. It is similar to Viennese schnitzel or Italian cotoletta, French côtelette de veau frite (or côtelette Menon), North and South American milanesa, Spanish cachopo and Japanese tonkatsu."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-kotlet-schabowy"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotlet_schabowy"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":9,"links":[["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Breadcrumbs","ingredient/breadcrumbs"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Pork loin","ingredient/pork-loin"],["Turkey meat","ingredient/turkey-meat"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Polonist","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"polonist.com","note":"Polish recipes and food culture from a Polish-born writer living abroad.","url":"https://www.polonist.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Polish Your Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"polishyourkitchen.com","note":"Long-running blog of traditional Polish home recipes.","url":"https://polishyourkitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Polish cuisine","links":[["Baba (cake)","dish/baba-cake"],["Bagel","dish/bagel"],["Borscht","dish/borscht"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Cabbage soup","dish/cabbage-soup"],["Cebularz","dish/cebularz"],["Cold beet soup","dish/cold-beet-soup"],["Gołąbki","dish/go-abki"],["Gugelhupf","dish/gugelhupf"],["Kasha","dish/kasha"],["Kibinai","dish/kibinai"],["Kolach (bread)","dish/kolach-bread"],["Krupnik (soup)","dish/krupnik-soup"],["Miodownik","dish/miodownik"],["Pancake","dish/pancake"],["Pease pudding","dish/pease-pudding"],["Pie","dish/pie"],["Potato pancake","dish/potato-pancake"],["Potato salad","dish/potato-salad"],["Racuchy","dish/racuchy"],["Rosół","dish/roso"],["Šakotis","dish/sakotis"],["Salceson","dish/salceson"],["Sauerkraut","dish/sauerkraut"],["Sorrel soup","dish/sorrel-soup"],["Steak tartare","dish/steak-tartare"],["Streuselkuchen","dish/streuselkuchen"],["Székelykáposzta","dish/szekelykaposzta"],["Tsvikli","dish/tsvikli"],["Uszka","dish/uszka"],["Zapiekanka","dish/zapiekanka"],["Zrazy","dish/zrazy"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/40/Kotlet_Schabowy.jpg/500px-Kotlet_Schabowy.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Dmitry Dzema","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kotlet_Schabowy.jpg"}},"ingredient/duck":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/duck","n":"Duck","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Duck is the common name for numerous species of waterfowl in the family Anatidae. Ducks are generally smaller and shorter-necked than swans and geese, which are members of the same family. Divided among several subfamilies, they are a form taxon; they do not represent a monophyletic group (the group of all descendants of a single common ancestral species), since swans and geese are not considered ducks. Ducks are mostly aquatic birds and may be found in both fresh water and sea water."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-duck"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duck"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Lontong dekem","dish/lontong-dekem"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Meat of ducks, fresh or chilled","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":6659915,"top":[["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",5333860],["Vietnam","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine",171807],["France","cuisine/french-cuisine",162230],["Thailand","cuisine/thai-cuisine",82051],["Taiwan","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine",79544],["South Korea","cuisine/korean-cuisine",71857],["Hungary","cuisine/hungarian-cuisine",69600],["Malaysia","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine",69359],["Poland","cuisine/polish-cuisine",66068],["Bangladesh","cuisine/bangladeshi-cuisine",62271],["Myanmar","cuisine/burmese-cuisine",59978],["United States of America","cuisine/american-cuisine",58484],["Egypt","cuisine/egyptian-cuisine",45006],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",44020],["Indonesia","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",42284]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Krupuk","ingredient/krupuk"],["Lontong","ingredient/lontong"],["Serundeng","ingredient/serundeng"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/bf/Bucephala-albeola-010.jpg/500px-Bucephala-albeola-010.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Mdf","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bucephala-albeola-010.jpg"}},"ingredient/tahu-goreng":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/tahu-goreng","n":"Tahu goreng","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Tahu goreng (Indonesian spelling) or tauhu goreng (Bruneian, Malaysian and Singaporean spelling) is a generic name for any type of fried tofu dish in the cuisines of Indonesia, Brunei, Malaysia and Singapore."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-tahu-goreng"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tahu_goreng"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Nasi lengko","dish/nasi-lengko"],["Nasi tempong","dish/nasi-tempong"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Peanut sauce","ingredient/peanut-sauce"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Allium tuberosum","ingredient/allium-tuberosum"],["Bawang goreng","ingredient/bawang-goreng"],["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Krupuk","ingredient/krupuk"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"],["Omelette","ingredient/omelette"],["Sweet soy sauce","ingredient/sweet-soy-sauce"],["Amaranth","ingredient/amaranth"],["Bakwan","ingredient/bakwan"],["Bird's eye chili","ingredient/bird-s-eye-chili"],["Cosmos","ingredient/cosmos"],["Holy basil","ingredient/holy-basil"],["Ikan goreng","ingredient/ikan-goreng"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/71/Indonesia_Tahu_goreng.JPG/500px-Indonesia_Tahu_goreng.JPG","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Banej","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indonesia_Tahu_goreng.JPG"}},"ingredient/amba-condiment":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/amba-condiment","n":"Amba (condiment)","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Amba or anba is a tangy condiment made with mango pickle. Created by Iraqi Jewish merchants in Bombay who purportedly shipped mangoes in vinegar back to Baghdad, Amba bears a resemblance to chutneys from South Asia. The spicy, sweet and sour sauce is typically made of pickled green mangoes, vinegar, salt, turmeric, chilies, and fenugreek. It is enjoyed in various Middle Eastern countries, particularly in Israel, as well as to a lesser extent in Europe and the United States (largely among the Iraqi, Jewish, and Assyrian diaspora)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-amba-condiment"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amba_(condiment)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Israeli cuisine","cuisine/israeli-cuisine",[["Sabich","dish/sabich"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Amba","ingredient/amba"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Hummus","ingredient/hummus"],["Israeli salad","ingredient/israeli-salad"],["Laffa","ingredient/laffa"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Pita","ingredient/pita"],["Salad","ingredient/salad"],["Tahini","ingredient/tahini"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/9/99/Amba.jpg","w":500,"h":388,"by":"Tatan48","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amba.jpg"}},"dish/tipatipa":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/tipatipa","n":"Tipatipa","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Tipatipa is a typical of Batak snack from Porsea, Toba Samosir, North Sumatra."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-tipatipa"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tipatipa"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycooking